# Modern Gentlemen — environment variables
# Copy to .env.local for local dev; set the same in Railway → Variables for prod.
# Never commit real values. See design_handoff_modern_gentlemen/06_SUPABASE.md.

# --- The site's own address ---
# Read by canonical tags, sitemap.xml, robots.txt and JSON-LD — everything a
# crawler reads back. REQUIRED FOR `npm run build`: `canonicalSiteUrl()` refuses
# to fall back to localhost when NODE_ENV is production, because a production
# build that quietly advertised http://localhost:3000 would tell Google every
# page lives on a machine it cannot reach. No trailing slash.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# --- Supabase (the single backend: content, products, auth, orders, storage) ---
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your_anon_key
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=your_service_role_key   # SERVER ONLY — bypasses RLS, never expose to the browser

# --- Stripe (payments; webhook writes paid orders into Supabase) ---
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_...
NEXT_PUBLIC_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=pk_test_...
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_...

# --- Background jobs ---
# Shared secret for POST /api/jobs/publish-scheduled, which fires documents whose
# scheduled_for has passed. Any long random string; the same value goes in the
# repository's JOBS_SECRET secret so the scheduled-publish workflow can call it.
# Unset, the route refuses every request rather than running unguarded.
JOBS_SECRET=a_long_random_string

# --- Newsletter ESP (optional; if not using a Supabase-only capture) ---
# ESP_API_KEY=your_klaviyo_or_mailchimp_key

# --- Provisioning the admin account (scripts/create-admin.ts, scripts/seed.ts) ---
# Which account receives the `admin` role. `create-admin.ts` needs both; the seed
# script reads the email only, to grant the role at the end of a seed run. Both
# have defaults, which is precisely why their absence went unnoticed: unset, the
# scripts quietly provision an account nobody meant to create rather than
# failing. Needs SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY above to be set as well.
SEED_ADMIN_EMAIL=welcome@moderngentlemen.co
SEED_ADMIN_PASSWORD=a_long_random_password

# --- Tests ---
# The signed-in E2E and admin-visual specs skip themselves when these are unset,
# which is not a failure and is easy to mistake for a pass. CI provisions the
# account with scripts/create-admin.ts and exports these two; locally you need an
# admin that already exists.
E2E_ADMIN_EMAIL=welcome@moderngentlemen.co
E2E_ADMIN_PASSWORD=your_admin_password

# The integration suite talks to a throwaway local stack, never to the live
# project — `npx supabase start` prints all three. tests/setup/integration.setup.ts
# throws a named error if any is missing, so this pair of blocks is the whole
# story for `npm run test:integration`.
SUPABASE_TEST_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54321
SUPABASE_TEST_ANON_KEY=the_local_stack_anon_key
SUPABASE_TEST_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=the_local_stack_service_role_key

# --- Product feed credentials (optional, one per authenticated source) ---
# A `product_sources` row names its credential rather than storing it, so the
# variable belongs to a *source* and not to the codebase — there is no fixed name
# to declare here, and `scripts/check-env.mjs` cannot see the read either, since
# it is a dynamic `process.env[ref]`.
#
# What holds the line instead is the FEED_ prefix: `resolveCredential` in
# lib/integrations/commerce refuses any reference that does not match
# ^FEED_[A-Z0-9_]+$. Without that rule an operator with `integration.write`
# could point a source at SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY and have the run send it, as
# a bearer token, to a URL they also chose. Name feed credentials accordingly:
# FEED_EXAMPLE_MERCHANT_TOKEN=the_merchants_api_token

# NOTE: Sanity and Shopify are NOT used (superseded by Supabase + Stripe).
